---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Dehumidifier for Basement",
    "url": "/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Draining a Dehumidifier Without a Floor Drain",
    "url": "/how-to-drain-a-dehumidifier-without-a-floor-drain/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How Much Electricity Does a Dehumidifier Use?",
    "url": "/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Where to Place a Dehumidifier in a Basement",
    "url": "/where-to-place-a-dehumidifier-in-a-basement/"
  },
  {
    "title": "What Size Dehumidifier for a Basement",
    "url": "/what-size-dehumidifier-for-basement/"
  },
  {
    "title": "What to Set a Basement Dehumidifier At",
    "url": "/what-should-a-basement-dehumidifier-be-set-at/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-29", modified: "2026-09-29" });
const title = "6 Best Dehumidifiers With a Pump for Basements Without a Floor Drain";
const description =
  "Six dehumidifiers with built-in pumps compared on lift height, hose, reliability and efficiency, plus when gravity drainage is the better choice.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "midea-cube-50-pint-pump",
    "name": "Midea Cube 50-Pint Dehumidifier with Pump",
    "category": "50 Pints (2019 DOE), Built-In Pump, Up to 4,500 Sq Ft",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.3,
    "reviewCount": 3000,
    "pros": [
      "Built-in pump rated to lift water about 16 feet vertically, enough to reach a first-floor sink or a basement window",
      "Unique nesting design stores compactly and extends to a much larger tank when you are not pumping",
      "ENERGY STAR certified, with the Cube line carrying Most Efficient recognition",
      "App and voice-assistant control for a unit that often lives in a room you rarely visit",
      "Lighter and smaller than a traditional 50-pint box unit"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The telescoping body and pump add parts that a basic unit does not have",
      "Pump hose length is not prominently published, so measure your run and buy extra tubing if needed"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best all-round pump dehumidifier for a typical basement: strong lift, good efficiency and a design that makes the bucket nearly irrelevant.",
    "description": "The Midea Cube looks unusual, and the design has a point. The unit telescopes: collapsed, it is a compact cube that is easy to store; extended, the lower section becomes a large water reservoir that holds far more than a typical bucket. The pump version adds a built-in condensate pump that Midea rates to push water about 16 feet vertically, which is enough to run a hose up and out of a basement window, over a laundry sink rim or into a drain line well above the floor. It is rated at 50 pints per day under the current 2019 DOE test at 65°F and 60% humidity and is sold for spaces up to about 4,500 square feet, which in practice means a large, damp basement rather than a whole house. It is ENERGY STAR certified, and Midea's Cube line has carried the program's Most Efficient recognition, which matters for a machine that may run several hours a day all summer. App control and voice-assistant support let you check the humidity and pump status without going downstairs. The trade-off is complexity. The telescoping body and the pump are more parts than a simple box dehumidifier has, and pumps are the component that most often fails on any portable. Keep the hose clear, clean the pump screen when you clean the filter, and it is the easiest recommendation here."
  },
  {
    "id": "frigidaire-ffap5033w1-pump",
    "name": "Frigidaire FFAP5033W1 50-Pint Dehumidifier with Built-In Pump",
    "category": "50 Pints (2019 DOE), Built-In Pump, ENERGY STAR",
    "badge": "Best Mainstream Pick",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.3,
    "reviewCount": 9000,
    "pros": [
      "Built-in pump plus a conventional gravity drain port, so you can switch methods if the pump ever fails",
      "Large 16.9-pint bucket gives a useful buffer if you ever run it without the hose",
      "ENERGY STAR certified",
      "Sold at nearly every major retailer, which makes replacement parts and hoses easy to find",
      "Familiar, simple controls that are hard to misconfigure"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Frigidaire does not prominently publish a pump lift height, so plan a conservative vertical run",
      "Heavier and bulkier than the Midea Cube at about 24 inches tall and 16 inches wide"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The safe, widely available choice, with the useful fallback of a gravity drain port if the pump ever quits.",
    "description": "Frigidaire's 50-pint pump model is the kind of dehumidifier you find at every big-box store, and that ubiquity is a feature rather than a knock. Replacement hoses, filters and service information are easy to find, and the design is familiar to anyone who has owned a dehumidifier. It removes 50 pints per day under the current 2019 DOE standard, it is ENERGY STAR certified, and it has both a built-in pump and a conventional gravity drain port. That second port is more valuable than it sounds. If the pump ever fails, and on portable dehumidifiers the pump is the part most likely to fail, you can switch to a floor drain or a separate condensate pump and keep running rather than being stuck with a unit that fills its bucket and stops. The 16.9-pint bucket is also larger than average, which gives you a buffer when you move the unit or run it temporarily without a hose. Frigidaire does not prominently publish the pump's lift height, so our advice is to plan a conservative run, keep the vertical rise to a sink or window modest, and test the setup on day one with the pump running. At about 24 by 16 by 12 inches it is bulkier than the Midea Cube, but in a basement corner that rarely matters."
  },
  {
    "id": "honeywell-tp70pwkn-pump",
    "name": "Honeywell TP70PWKN 50-Pint Dehumidifier with Pump",
    "category": "50 Pints (2019 DOE, formerly 70), Built-In Pump, Up to 4,000 Sq Ft",
    "badge": "Best for Cooler Basements",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.3,
    "reviewCount": 3500,
    "pros": [
      "Built-in pump drains vertically or horizontally up to about 15 feet",
      "Rated for low-temperature operation down to about 41°F, useful in unfinished basements",
      "ENERGY STAR certified, with a 14-pint tank as a backup",
      "Auto restart after power outages and a 24-hour programmable timer",
      "Clear labeling of both the old 70-pint and current 50-pint ratings"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Capacity drops sharply near its low-temperature limit, like every compressor portable",
      "Bulkier than newer designs and fairly loud on high fan"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A dependable pump unit for unfinished basements that run cool, as long as you understand what low-temperature operation actually means.",
    "description": "The Honeywell TP70PWKN is a useful example of the pint-rating change. It was sold for years as a 70-pint dehumidifier, and it is now labeled as a 50-pint unit under the 2019 DOE test. The machine did not get weaker; the test got cooler and more realistic. Honeywell deserves credit for printing both numbers on the box, which helps anyone comparing it with older reviews. The built-in pump drains water vertically or horizontally up to about 15 feet, which covers most basement windows and laundry sinks, and there is a 14-pint tank as a fallback. Honeywell rates it for operation down to about 41°F, and that makes it a better fit than many portables for an unfinished basement that runs cool in spring and fall. Be realistic about what that rating means, though. Every compressor dehumidifier loses a large share of its capacity as the air cools toward its minimum, and at the low end the unit will spend more time defrosting. It is ENERGY STAR certified, it restarts automatically after a power cut, and it has a 24-hour timer. It is a little bulkier and louder than the newest designs, but it is a well-proven pump unit with a long track record, and it is a good choice for a cool, unfinished basement."
  },
  {
    "id": "ge-aper50lz-pump",
    "name": "GE 50-Pint ENERGY STAR Dehumidifier with Built-In Pump (APER50LZ)",
    "category": "50 Pints (2019 DOE), Built-In Pump, 16-Ft Hose",
    "badge": "Best Smart Controls",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.2,
    "reviewCount": 1500,
    "pros": [
      "16-foot hose included, so you can reach a sink or window without buying extra tubing",
      "Smart Dry mode adjusts fan speed to the difference between the set and actual humidity",
      "Automatic defrost cycles the compressor off while the fan clears the coil",
      "ENERGY STAR certified",
      "Compact footprint at about 24 by 15 by 11.5 inches"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Fewer owner reviews than the Frigidaire and Honeywell, so long-term pump reliability is less proven",
      "Premium pricing compared with similar 50-pint pump units"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A well-equipped pump dehumidifier that ships with everything you need for the drain run, at a price that is fair rather than cheap.",
    "description": "GE's 50-pint pump model is one of the few that ships with a hose long enough to be useful straight out of the box. The included 16-foot hose means you can usually reach a laundry sink or a basement window without a trip to the hardware store, and that is exactly where many first-time pump installs stall. It removes 50 pints per day under the 2019 DOE standard, it is ENERGY STAR certified, and it includes GE's Smart Dry mode, which compares the humidity you set with the humidity in the room and adjusts fan speed to match. In practice that means it runs quietly once a basement is close to its target rather than blasting at full speed. Automatic defrost shuts the compressor off and keeps the fan running when frost builds on the coil, which helps in a cool basement. The unit also sounds an alarm when its internal water storage is full, which is an important signal on a pump unit because it usually means the pump or hose has stopped moving water. At about 24 by 15 by 11.5 inches, it has a smaller footprint than most 50-pint units. The main reservation is that it has fewer owner reviews than the Frigidaire or Honeywell, so its long-term pump reliability is less well documented. It is fairly priced for what you get, not a bargain."
  },
  {
    "id": "homelabs-4500-pump",
    "name": "hOmeLabs 4,500 Sq. Ft. ENERGY STAR Dehumidifier with Pump",
    "category": "50 Pints (2019 DOE), Built-In Pump, 16.4-Ft Hose",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 6000,
    "pros": [
      "Usually the lowest price of the 50-pint ENERGY STAR pump units",
      "Comes with a 16.4-foot drain hose for the pump, so the drain run is covered",
      "Very large owner base on Amazon, which makes troubleshooting help easy to find",
      "Simple push-button pump activation and continuous-drain operation",
      "Solid real-world performance in large, damp basements"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Pump lift height is not clearly published, so test your vertical run before relying on it",
      "Build quality is a step below Frigidaire and Midea, and owners report more variation unit to unit"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The value pick when you need a pump and do not want to pay for app control, with the caveat that you should test the pump the day it arrives.",
    "description": "hOmeLabs sells one of the most popular budget dehumidifiers on Amazon, and the pump version keeps the formula: 50 pints per day under the 2019 DOE test, ENERGY STAR certification and a price that usually undercuts the big appliance brands. Unlike some competitors, it ships with a 16.4-foot pump hose, so you can run water to a sink or out a window without buying extra tubing. You press a pump button to switch from the bucket to pump drainage, and from then on it runs continuously. In large, damp basements, owners consistently report that it pulls humidity down to the 45% to 50% range and keeps it there. The trade-offs are the usual ones for a value brand. hOmeLabs does not clearly publish a pump lift height, and the build quality is a step below Midea and Frigidaire, with more owner reports of pumps that quit after a season or two of heavy use. Our advice is simple: on the day it arrives, set up the full hose run you intend to use, fill the tank and confirm the pump clears it. If it cannot handle your vertical rise, return it while you can. If it can, it is a lot of dehumidifier for the money, and the large owner base means answers to most problems are only a search away."
  },
  {
    "id": "alorair-sentinel-hd35p-pump",
    "name": "AlorAir Sentinel HD35P Dehumidifier with Pump",
    "category": "35 PPD at AHAM, 70 PPD Saturation, Built-In Pump, Works to 33.8°F",
    "badge": "Best for Crawl Spaces and Cold Basements",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.3,
    "reviewCount": 700,
    "pros": [
      "Rated to operate down to 33.8°F, far below any portable in this guide",
      "Built-in condensate pump plus a separate gravity hose, so it works with or without a drain",
      "ENERGY STAR certified with a metal, low-profile body designed for crawl spaces",
      "Designed for years of unattended continuous running rather than a single season",
      "Relatively quiet at a listed 55 dB maximum"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Its 35-pint AHAM rating means less capacity than the 50-pint portables in a warm basement",
      "Costs noticeably more than a portable of similar rated capacity"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Skip the portables if the space is a crawl space or a basement that runs cold, and buy this instead.",
    "description": "Every other unit in this guide is a portable dehumidifier designed for a basement that someone uses. The HD35P is different: a compact, metal-bodied crawl space dehumidifier with a built-in condensate pump, and it is the right pick when the space is colder or harder to reach than a typical basement. AlorAir rates it at 35 pints per day at AHAM conditions and 70 at saturation for spaces up to about 1,000 square feet. The AHAM number is measured at 80°F rather than the 65°F used for portables, so it is not directly comparable, and in a warm finished basement a 50-pint portable will outwork it. Where it wins is in the cold. It is rated to keep running down to 33.8°F, while portables typically bottom out in the low 40s and lose much of their capacity well before that. It is ENERGY STAR certified, it includes both a pump hose and a longer gravity hose, and its listed maximum noise level is 55 dB. For an encapsulated crawl space or a cold, unfinished basement where the unit will run unattended for years, it is a better tool than any portable here. If your crawl space is larger or wetter, see our full guide to crawl space units for the bigger models."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Dehumidifiers With Pump",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-dehumidifier-with-pump/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How high can a dehumidifier pump push water?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most built-in pumps on 50-pint portable dehumidifiers are rated for roughly 15 to 16 feet of vertical lift. Midea rates its Cube pump at about 16 feet and Honeywell rates the TP70PWKN at about 15. Treat those as maximums, and plan real installs at about two-thirds of the rating, especially with long horizontal runs or several bends in the hose."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a dehumidifier with a pump worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, if your only drain is above the unit, such as a laundry sink or basement window. A pump lets the dehumidifier run continuously without anyone emptying a bucket. If you have a floor drain or sump pit lower than the unit, a gravity hose is simpler and more reliable, and you can skip the pump."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does my dehumidifier pump keep stopping?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The usual cause is a clog. Slimy biofilm builds up on the pump inlet screen and inside the hose, slowing flow until the unit reports a full tank or pump error. Kinked, frozen or submerged hose ends cause the same symptom. Clean the screen, flush or replace the hose and make sure the discharge end is clear and above any water."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use a garden hose with a dehumidifier pump?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Generally not. Built-in pumps use a small-diameter hose that attaches to a specific fitting, and many include a long hose in the box. A garden hose connection on a dehumidifier is normally the gravity drain port, not the pump outlet. Use the supplied pump hose or an exact replacement of the same size."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does the pump use a lot of electricity?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. The pump runs only for a few seconds each time the internal reservoir fills, and its energy use is tiny compared with the compressor and fan. A 50-pint unit draws roughly 500 to 600 watts while running, almost all of it from the compressor. Choose an ENERGY STAR model to keep overall running costs down."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size dehumidifier with a pump do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most basements, a 50-pint unit rated under the 2019 DOE standard is the right size. Nearly all pump dehumidifiers are 50-pint models for that reason. Very wet basements or humid climates need that capacity even in 1,500 to 2,000 square feet. For crawl spaces, use a purpose-built crawl space unit sized by floor area instead."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a pump dehumidifier drain out a window?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Run the hose up to the window sill and out through a small gap or a hole in a window insert, then direct the end so water lands at least a few feet from the foundation on ground that slopes away. In winter, make sure the outdoor end cannot freeze, or switch to a sink or indoor drain for the cold months."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Basement Waterproofing",
    tags: [
      "best dehumidifier with pump",
      "dehumidifier with pump",
      "dehumidifier with built-in pump",
      "basement dehumidifier",
      "dehumidifier continuous drain"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            6 Pump Units Compared
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Dehumidifiers With a Pump</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Six built-in pump dehumidifiers compared on the specs that decide whether a pump install works: vertical lift, hose, backup drainage and how the pump holds up.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#midea-cube-50-pint-pump"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Do You Need a Pump?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="12.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#pump-or-gravity" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Do You Need a Pump?</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#lift-height" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Lift Height and Hose Runs</a>
          <a href="#failure-modes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. How Pumps Fail</a>
          <a href="#pint-ratings" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Pint Ratings and Sizing</a>
          <a href="#energy" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. ENERGY STAR and Running Costs</a>
          <a href="#placement" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Placement and Setup</a>
          <a href="#cold-spaces" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Cold Basements and Crawl Spaces</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A built-in pump is the answer when the only drain is above your dehumidifier. It is also the part most likely to fail, so buy a unit with real lift, plan a short and simple hose run, and keep a gravity route as a backup if you can.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dehumidifier with a built-in pump solves one specific problem: getting water out of a basement that has no floor drain at or below the unit. Instead of relying on gravity, a small pump inside the machine pushes condensate up through a hose to a sink, a window or a drain line above the floor. That is the feature we evaluated first, and it narrowed the field quickly.
          </p>

          <p>
            We looked only at units whose pump is built in and designed for continuous use, not models that merely offer a gravity drain port. We then compared them on the numbers that decide whether a pump install works: the rated vertical lift, the length of hose included, whether a gravity drain port remains available as a backup and what owners report about pump reliability after a season or more of running.
          </p>

          <p>
            Capacity and efficiency came next. Every portable here is rated at 50 pints per day under the 2019 Department of Energy test at 65°F and 60% humidity, which is the current, realistic standard for basement use. We favored ENERGY STAR certified models because a basement dehumidifier can run many hundreds of hours a year, and efficiency differences add up. We also noted which units publish a low-temperature operating limit, since many pump buyers have unfinished basements that run cool.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we included one unit that is not a portable at all. Crawl spaces and cold basements are exactly the places where a pump is most needed and where portables most often fail, so we added a compact crawl space unit with a built-in pump for readers in that situation.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do have a floor drain close by, you may not need a pump at all, and our guide to the <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">best basement dehumidifiers</a> covers excellent units without one. Pumps add convenience, but they also add a part that can fail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pump-or-gravity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Do You Actually Need a Pump?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first question is not which pump dehumidifier to buy but whether you need a pump at all. Gravity drainage is simpler, quieter and more reliable, because there is nothing to fail. If you can run a hose downhill from the dehumidifier to a floor drain, sump pit or utility sink drain, a unit without a pump will do the same job with fewer moving parts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gravity drainage has one firm requirement: the hose has to fall the whole way. Any section that rises, sags or loops will trap water, and the flow will stop. That usually means the drain must be lower than the unit's drain port, which on most portables is only a few inches off the floor. You can raise the dehumidifier onto a sturdy stand to gain a foot or so of fall, and that trick alone solves many installs.
          </p>

          <p>
            You need a pump when the only available drain is above the unit: a laundry sink, a basement window, a condensate line or a drain on the floor above. You also need one when the nearest floor drain is so far away that a gravity hose would cross walkways or would not maintain its slope. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-drain-a-dehumidifier-without-a-floor-drain/">how to drain a dehumidifier without a floor drain</a> walks through every option, including external condensate pumps that you can add to a unit without one.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a middle path worth knowing about. A dehumidifier with both a built-in pump and a gravity port gives you a fallback. If the pump fails, you can switch to gravity or add an external pump rather than going back to emptying a bucket. The Frigidaire in this guide has that feature, and we consider it a genuine advantage.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you choose a pump, accept that it is a maintenance item. Pump dehumidifiers need their hose and pump inlet kept clean, and they reward owners who check them occasionally. If that sounds like a chore, spend the effort instead on finding a gravity route.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Use gravity when you can, a pump when you must.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A downhill hose to a floor drain or sump pit has no parts to fail. Buy a pump model when the only drain is above the unit, and prefer one that keeps a gravity port as a backup.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

