        <div id="lift-height" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Lift Height, Hose Runs and Where the Water Goes
          </h2>

          <p>
            The key pump spec is vertical lift, the height the pump can push water above the unit. Most 50-pint pump dehumidifiers are rated for roughly 15 to 16 feet. Midea rates the Cube pump at about 16 feet, Honeywell rates the TP70PWKN at about 15 feet, and GE and hOmeLabs include hoses of 16 feet or more. Lift ratings are measured under ideal conditions, so plan to use no more than about two-thirds of the rated figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            That still leaves plenty of room for real installs. A typical basement window sits 6 to 7 feet above the floor, and a laundry sink rim is about 3 feet. A drain line in the floor joists above is usually 8 to 9 feet up. All of those are comfortably within range. Where people get into trouble is sending the hose up to a first-floor sink and then across a long horizontal run, where the combination of height and hose length adds resistance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hose matters as much as the pump. Use the hose supplied or one of the same inner diameter, keep it as short as practical, avoid kinks and tight bends and secure it so it cannot slip out of the sink. Where it discharges into a sink, clip it to the rim so the end sits above the water line and cannot siphon or fall out. Where it goes outside, keep the end clear of the foundation and protect it from freezing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think about the discharge point as a system, not an afterthought. Water pumped into a laundry sink is fine as long as the sink drains freely. Water pushed out a window should land at least a few feet from the foundation on ground that slopes away, or it will find its way back into the basement you are trying to dry.
          </p>

