        <div id="failure-modes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Built-In Pumps Fail and How to Prevent It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Built-in pumps are the component most likely to fail on a portable dehumidifier, and most failures follow a few predictable patterns. Understanding them turns a pump unit from a gamble into a reliable machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most common problem is a clog. Dehumidifier condensate is clean at first, but it runs over coils and through a reservoir that stays warm and wet, and over time a slimy biofilm forms. That slime collects on the pump inlet screen and inside the hose. Flow slows, the pump runs longer, and eventually the unit reports a full tank or a pump error and stops. Cleaning the pump screen and flushing the hose every few months prevents most of these failures.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second is a blocked or frozen discharge. A hose that is kinked, crushed behind furniture or run outside in freezing weather stops flowing, and the pump either runs dry or shuts down. Route the hose where it cannot be stepped on, avoid tight bends and bring outdoor lines inside or insulate them before winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is air lock and siphoning. If the discharge end sits below the water in a sink, or the hose loops downward after rising, water can siphon back or trap air. Keep the discharge end above the waterline and make the path simple: straight up, then gently across and down.
          </p>

          <p>
            The last is simple wear. Small pumps that cycle every few minutes for months on end eventually wear out, which is one more reason to prefer a unit with a gravity port as backup. Test the pump on day one by running the unit until it pumps, and check it again at the start of each humid season. If it fails, an external condensate pump can take over in most cases.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pint-ratings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Pint Ratings and Sizing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every portable dehumidifier sold since 2019 is rated under the Department of Energy test at 65°F and 60% relative humidity. Older models were rated at 80°F and 60%, and because cooler air holds less water, the same machine now carries a smaller number. The Honeywell in this guide is a clear example: it was sold for years as a 70-pint unit and is now labeled 50 pints. The machine did not change.
          </p>

          <p>
            That matters when you compare reviews or older recommendations. A 70-pint dehumidifier from an older article is roughly equivalent to a 50-pint unit today, and a 50-pint unit from before 2019 is closer to 35 pints under the current test. When in doubt, look for the phrase 2019 DOE standard on the box or listing.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most basements, a 50-pint unit is the right size. The large coverage figures on the box, such as 4,000 or 4,500 square feet, assume a moderately damp space and good air circulation. A very wet basement, one with a history of water intrusion or one in a humid coastal climate will need that capacity even at 1,500 to 2,000 square feet. It is also why nearly all pump dehumidifiers are 50-pint units: pumps are most useful on the machines that produce the most water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size up rather than down. A dehumidifier that is slightly oversized reaches the target humidity and cycles off, while one that is undersized runs constantly, wears out faster and never quite gets there. Our guide to the <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">best basement dehumidifiers</a> covers sizing in more depth, including when a whole-house system makes more sense.
          </p>

