        <div id="energy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. ENERGY STAR and Running Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 50-pint dehumidifier typically draws around 500 to 600 watts while the compressor runs. In a damp basement during summer, it may run 8 to 16 hours a day, and at typical U.S. electricity rates that works out to somewhere around a dollar or two a day at peak. Over a humid season, efficiency is worth real money.
          </p>

          <p>
            ENERGY STAR certification tells you a unit removes more water per kilowatt-hour than the federal minimum requires, measured as integrated energy factor in liters per kWh. All of the portables in this guide are ENERGY STAR certified, and Midea's Cube line has also carried the program's Most Efficient recognition. Uncertified units are sometimes cheaper to buy but can cost more to run within a season or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            The pump itself uses very little electricity. It runs for a few seconds each time the internal reservoir fills, and its consumption is a rounding error next to the compressor. Do not let the pump put you off an otherwise efficient unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bigger levers are setpoint and air sealing. Setting the unit to 50% rather than 40% can cut run time substantially, and 50% is low enough to prevent mold and musty odors. Sealing obvious air leaks and fixing water intrusion reduces the load before the dehumidifier ever sees it. Our breakdown of <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/">how much electricity a dehumidifier uses</a> has the full math for estimating your own costs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Placement and Setup for a Pump Unit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Placement matters more with a pump unit than without one, because you are balancing two goals: putting the dehumidifier where it dries the most air and putting it where the hose run is short and simple.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with airflow. The unit should sit away from walls with at least a foot of clearance around the intake and exhaust, in a central location or near the dampest part of the basement. Do not tuck it into a closet or behind boxes, where it will mostly re-dry its own exhaust. Our guide on <a href="/where-to-place-a-dehumidifier-in-a-basement/">where to place a dehumidifier in a basement</a> covers the common layouts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then plan the hose. Pick the drain point first, whether that is a laundry sink, window or drain line, and position the unit so the hose goes straight up and gently across with no loops. A short, simple run is more reliable than a long one, and the difference in drying is usually small if the unit is still reasonably central.
          </p>

          <p>
            On setup day, fill the reservoir by running the unit until the pump cycles, and watch the full cycle. Check that water reaches the discharge point quickly and that the end of the hose stays put. Check again after a few days to catch slow leaks at connections and make sure the unit has not shifted. Set the humidity target around 45% to 50%, turn on auto restart if it is a menu option and put a separate hygrometer on the far side of the basement to confirm the reading.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, make the filter and pump screen easy to reach. A unit that is awkward to service is a unit that does not get serviced.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cold-spaces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Cold Basements and Crawl Spaces
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many pump buyers have exactly the kind of space where portables struggle: an unfinished basement that runs in the 50s for much of the year, or a crawl space under part of the house. Pumps are especially useful in those spaces, because they rarely have a convenient floor drain, but temperature changes which dehumidifier is the right tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            Portable compressor dehumidifiers work well in the mid-60s and above. Below that, their coils start to frost, they spend more time defrosting and their real output drops well below the rated figure. Most portables are rated to operate into the low 40s, but a unit that can run at 41°F is not delivering its rated capacity there. In a basement that runs cool only in spring and fall, a good portable like the Honeywell is fine. In a space that stays cold, it will underperform.
          </p>

          <p>
            Crawl spaces are a different category. They are cool, cramped and rarely visited, and the dehumidifier may need to run year after year without anyone checking on it. That is why purpose-built crawl space units use low-temperature coils, metal housings, long warranties and built-in pumps or drain connections. The AlorAir HD35P in this guide is one example, rated to run down to 33.8°F.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your project is a crawl space, read our full guide to the <a href="/best-crawl-space-dehumidifier/">best crawl space dehumidifiers</a> before buying anything. It covers why the space needs to be sealed first, how to size a unit and which models handle cold air best.
          </p>
        </div>

