          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a pump when gravity would work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pump adds a part that can clog or fail. If you can run a hose downhill to a floor drain, sump pit or utility sink drain, even by raising the unit on a sturdy stand, gravity drainage is simpler and more reliable. Buy a pump model only when the drain point is genuinely above the unit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning the hose run at full rated lift</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pump lift ratings are measured under ideal conditions. A long hose, tight bends and a high discharge point all add resistance. Plan to use no more than about two-thirds of the rated lift, keep the hose short and simple and test the complete run on day one while you can still return the unit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never cleaning the pump screen and hose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Condensate grows slimy biofilm over time, and that slime clogs pump inlets and hoses. It is the most common cause of pump failures and full-tank errors. Clean the pump screen when you clean the filter, flush the hose every few months and replace any hose that shows buildup inside.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the discharge end drop into water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A hose end that sits below the waterline in a sink can siphon, trap air or slip out entirely. Clip the end to the sink rim so it sits above the water, or discharge outdoors well away from the foundation, and protect outdoor lines from freezing in winter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a portable in a cold crawl space</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Portable dehumidifiers lose most of their capacity in cool air and are not built for years of unattended running. In a crawl space or a basement that stays in the 50s, choose a purpose-built low-temperature unit with a pump or drain connection instead of hoping a portable will cope.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
