          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How high can a dehumidifier pump push water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most built-in pumps on 50-pint portable dehumidifiers are rated for roughly 15 to 16 feet of vertical lift. Midea rates its Cube pump at about 16 feet and Honeywell rates the TP70PWKN at about 15. Treat those as maximums, and plan real installs at about two-thirds of the rating, especially with long horizontal runs or several bends in the hose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a dehumidifier with a pump worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if your only drain is above the unit, such as a laundry sink or basement window. A pump lets the dehumidifier run continuously without anyone emptying a bucket. If you have a floor drain or sump pit lower than the unit, a gravity hose is simpler and more reliable, and you can skip the pump.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my dehumidifier pump keep stopping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The usual cause is a clog. Slimy biofilm builds up on the pump inlet screen and inside the hose, slowing flow until the unit reports a full tank or pump error. Kinked, frozen or submerged hose ends cause the same symptom. Clean the screen, flush or replace the hose and make sure the discharge end is clear and above any water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a garden hose with a dehumidifier pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally not. Built-in pumps use a small-diameter hose that attaches to a specific fitting, and many include a long hose in the box. A garden hose connection on a dehumidifier is normally the gravity drain port, not the pump outlet. Use the supplied pump hose or an exact replacement of the same size.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the pump use a lot of electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The pump runs only for a few seconds each time the internal reservoir fills, and its energy use is tiny compared with the compressor and fan. A 50-pint unit draws roughly 500 to 600 watts while running, almost all of it from the compressor. Choose an ENERGY STAR model to keep overall running costs down.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size dehumidifier with a pump do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most basements, a 50-pint unit rated under the 2019 DOE standard is the right size. Nearly all pump dehumidifiers are 50-pint models for that reason. Very wet basements or humid climates need that capacity even in 1,500 to 2,000 square feet. For crawl spaces, use a purpose-built crawl space unit sized by floor area instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a pump dehumidifier drain out a window?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Run the hose up to the window sill and out through a small gap or a hole in a window insert, then direct the end so water lands at least a few feet from the foundation on ground that slopes away. In winter, make sure the outdoor end cannot freeze, or switch to a sink or indoor drain for the cold months.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
